  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 152 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThe coldest day of this stretch is likely to be Friday as fresh cold \nadvection behind the reinforcing front sends 850MB temperatures down \nto about -5C or about 10C below normal for this time of year. \nDespite more sunshine on Friday than on Thursday, temperatures area \nwide top out only in the mid 40s. As the surface high pressure area \ngets closer this weekend we'll see winds lighten up and cold \nadvection cease. Nighttime lows will be their coldest if we can get \na clear, calm night. Right now the best potential for that is Friday \nnight into Saturday morning when latest NBM forecast calls for 20s \narea wide. Saturday night could be just as cold if cloud cover holds \noff and we see another clear, calm night.\n\nThe surface high gradually nudges east through the Great Lakes early \nnext week with some flavor of upper ridging forecast by all guidance \nto develop over the center of the country next week. How quickly our \ntemperatures rebound will depend on how slow the surface high is to \nexit to the east and allow for a southerly low level flow to \ndevelop. Among the ensemble guidance, the earliest warm up back to \nabove normal temperatures locally is on Monday while some members \nhold on to below normal temperatures through at least Wednesday.\n\nRain chances are minimal through the middle of next week as \nnorthwest flow aloft promotes subsidence and suppresses moisture to \nthe south and west. The only possible exception is a southern stream \nshortwave passing by to our south Sunday into Monday. Among the 12Z \nLREF guidance, up to 30 percent of members produce measurable \nrainfall in parts of our forecast area during this time period, \ndown about 10 to 20 percent from the 00Z run.",
